ould have looked like if we work  backwards from a modern cell and  forgive the simplicity of this cartoon  as well this is  you know one of the processes we see in  the modern cell it's as you know the  central dogma  and these are composed of different  processes made up of biopolymers and  biomolecules and catalyzed by  biomolecules so what we want to know is  on early earth or in an early cell which  of these processes were still essential  so  for example it's very likely that  proteins did not exist on early earth  they're too large they're  they're folding is too difficult to have  achieved  and also it's very likely that our dna  couldn't have existed really on an early  earth so this leads many to  postulate that the first cells on early  earth contain rna and this is  specifically  um leads to a uh  origin of life hypothesis which suggests  that rna is the first and most important  biomolecule of life  this hypothesis is called the rna world  hypothesis and it postulates that  because rna can store and pass down  genetic information  because it's similar in structure to dna  and also because rna can fold into  specific structures that can catalyze  chemical processes  in the form of ribozymes similar to  proteins that rna was the first and most  important biomolecule on early earth and  while there's been a lot of research  in this  field and a lot of progress has been  made  there's still a number of outstanding  questions  in the rna world for example how the rna  could be synthesized how it polymer  polymerizes how it's prevented from  hydrolyzing there's many questions that  researchers are answering to this day  and there are also hypotheses that rna  wasn't the only biomolecule that existed  on early earth for example  some researchers have postulated that  dna and rna could have  coexisted on early earth maybe in a  hybrid form  and based on the structure of the  ribosome as we know it today it's also  been postulated that perhaps rna and  peptides co-evolved so instead of a  purely rna world you might have had  something like an rna peptide or an rna  protein world  and so what all this is just saying is  that we're not exactly sure exactly  what was in this early cell and it's  plausible that all of these or even more  types of molecules existed in or around  these cells so let's assume from now on  that we have access to a number of  different types of biomolecules for  building this primitive cell  the next thing i want to focus on is  this boundary so this boundary is really  important and defines a cell from other  cells specifically it makes the cell  defined as a compartment and  compartments are really essential to  origins of life  compartments segregate and con  concentrate reactants they promote  chemical reactions  promote evolution prevent fusion of  genetic materials many many different  important functions on early earth  and  you know modern cells they're made up  mostly of a phospholipid membrane that's  the boundary it's been postulated that  perhaps early cells that are membrane  bound were made up of fatty acids rather  than phospholipids because they're much  easier to synthesize  using prebiotic chemistry  and what's available back on early earth  due to there being only one chain rather  than two  and so one further question is that  can you actually make a boundary that's  even simpler than fatty acids that that  doesn't even require that type of  bilayer and so what we're interested in  is seeing whether such types of  compartments could have existed and  whether they could form or function as  early cells or protocells  so  one way is through liquid liquid phase  separation for those who are familiar  with this um you might have heard about  this if you haven't it's actually one  way to form droplets  in solution these droplets are are  membraneless but they're still liquid  and they provide compartmentalization  without a membrane they allow exchange  of molecules between phases and in fact  they're present in modern biology so  there's a phenomenon called biological  phase separation which results in  formation of membraneless organelles in  the cell these organelles you know there  are some some of them are called for  example  nuage granules or stress granules maybe  chromatin is even considered one of  these and these are membraneless  organelles in the cell that are really  important for controlling biological  processes and it's been postulated that  because  you know most cells or almost all cells  have this  type of face separation  inside to make these membraneless  organelles that perhaps they're an  ancient invention and that phase  separation was also used to make  compartments on early earth  and so here let me move on to a couple  case studies of origins of life research  and let me start with the biopolymer  case so this is a case study on  primitive liquid crystal coaster rates  so you might have heard of liquid  crystals before they're present in  tvs or displays  and in fact you can actually make liquid  crystals out of dna so this is in  collaboration with  tommaso fraccia who's a researcher at  espci in paris  and so his previous research showed that  if you took short dna oligomers that  were self-complementary such as this  sequence shown here  they can actually form these duplexes  that aggregate end to end they stack  together and they form these long rods  and these rods will bundle together and  after drying after increasing the  concentration of the dna significantly  the structure that results is this  columnar liquid crystal structure  shown in this polarization micro  microscopy image on the right  so these structures are you know  somewhat liquid they have liquid and  solid characteristics and you can  visualize them through polarization  microscopy because of their super  molecular chirality  so we postulated that perhaps  it would be interesting if for example  a primitive compartment like one of  those phase separated systems could  actually house one of these liquid  crystal structures because they've shown  the ability to affect electrochemistry  or regio chemistry or regioselectivity  so what we did was we knew that  you know when nucleic acids like dna  and when peptides  like cationic peptides are mixed and  bind together they actually can form  these liquid liquid phase separated  structures so we took pll polylysine  about 250 residues long  polydisperse mixed it with high  concentrations of this dna ligamer  and we were able to find conditions in  which these  structures these nice membraneless  droplets or coastervates co-localized  with this dna crystal liquid crystal  structure shown on the bottoms through  the polarization microscopy so we we  believe that this dna liquid crystal  structure is formed through this  end-to-end stacking interaction of dna  and we actually found that these  structures the co-localized structure of  the liquid crystal coast survey only  exists in a very narrow range of  salinity for example when we varied the  salinity we found that at low  concentrations of sodium chloride  precipitation occurred whereas at higher  concentrations of sodium chloride only  droplets formed without liquid crystal  characteristics  or no droplets could form at all and we  believe that this is because  the ionic strength of the solution is  really important for this interaction if  there's lower amounts of salt the  peptide dna binding interaction may be  too strong forming precipitation or  solid-like particles whereas at higher  salt concentrations it  makes these structures these  interactions between the peptide and the  dna weaker suggesting that you know when  when the peptide and dna can no longer  interact for example at one molar sodium  chloride these droplets can't form as  well  we next wanted to hypothesize test our  hypothesis  uh as to whether this end-to-end  interaction is indeed what's forming  these liquid crystal structures so we  did the same experiment but with a  slightly different dna sequence this  time we included a 2t base overhang so  that this stacking process could not  occur  and we found that through all of the  different conditions we tested  in no  single case did we find liquid crystal  structures we did find many  conditions where these droplets could  form these phase separated droplets  could form but liquid crystals could not  be observed and this shows that this  end-to-end stacking interaction is  really essential for these liquid  crystals to form  we then next wanted to study the um  what happens if we subjected these  liquid crystal coaster rates to  processes or environmental processes  that might have existed on earlier so  one of them is dehydration  it's been proposed that dehydration  could be one way to  you know effect  different chemical reactions on early  earth you'll see this in the second part  as well and so we subjected these  systems to drying and so what you see  here is that throughout the drying  process  you can see these structures go from  these solid-like structures  transitioning to these  liquid crystal  coaster rate like structures to just a  simple coastervate and then to  a disassembled structure i'll play this  video again and we believe this is the  case because similar to the salinity  titration  dehydrating the solution actually up  concentrates the sodium chloride in the  solution perhaps which suggests that the  this phase transition follows a similar  phase transition as what we observed in  the sodium chloride titration case  next we started again and we wanted to  see the  reaction of when we heated these samples  so  we actually heated these samples up to  about 60 degrees and we can see a  transition from  this solid-like structure to a liquid  crystal coaster rate structure  furthermore further heating resulted in  disassembly of these liquid crystal  structures  resulting only in these non-liquid  crystal coaster rates  and then upon decreasing the temperature  we noticed that at 40 degrees on the way  down these liquid crystal classic rates  started to pop up again and we saw a few  different types of liquid crystal  coastal rates but also we saw that the  the population was also heterogeneous  some of them were still  you know typical droplets without liquid  crystal characteristics some of them had  liquid crystals inside  and we found that upon decreasing at  further to about 10 degrees that almost  all of these droplets  reconstituted this  liquid crystal character if we compare  between these before and after heating  the structures are pretty different and  we think that this is because this  heating process is basically akin to an  annealing process and of course  annealing is used to  allow in in you know dna and rna case to  allow the strands to bind completely to  find the most stable thermodynamic  conformation and we believe that in this  case perhaps the right figure here is  this is what the most stable  conformation is for the system  and so we believe that what's happening  is we have these liquid crystal  structures here  with increasing heat and salinity  you have these you know dna long dna  rods and teal and these  red strands of peptides bound together  the rods the dna rods are in this packed  hexagonal pattern upon increasing heat  and salinity these rods start to come  together  come apart  causing some structural change and then  the rods start to disassemble these into  um  you know d-stack and so you lose this  liquid crystal characteristic but you  retain the droplets and then further  increasing heat and salinity results in  disassembly of everything and you result  in no droplets  so for those who are interested in  learning more details some of these  studies are published you can see  one here  and one here as well i'd highly  encourage you to take a look if you're  interested in the details  next i'd like to um talk about our other  side non-biopolymer side of origins of  life research and here we focus on  membraneless polyester droplets  so here we're building from the bottom  up and you see  we're interested in building this  primitive compartment from the available  chemicals on early earth and it's  actually known that there's many ways by  which biomolecules could have formed on  early earth through you know meteorites  or atmospheric discharge  but we also find that together with  biomolecules that there's also many  non-biomolecules synthesized at the same  time for example murchison meteorite has  tons and tons of molecules inside  and if you analyze the miller ureaction  or atmospheric discharge  reaction products you also get tons and  tons of different molecules not all of  these are biomolecules  this suggests that non-biomolecules were  just as or even more abundant than  biomolecules on early earth so we're  interested in understanding how  biomolecules could catalyze how  non-biomolecules could have catalyzed  life's emergence  the system we like to use are alpha  hydroxy acids they're monomers similar  in structure to amino acids but with an  amine group that's replaced by a  hydroxyl group and what's great about  these is that through simple drying  these monomers can form polyester  polymers shown on the bottom here  so we wanted to create these polymers  these polyesters and see if they could  you know assemble into something useful  for the origin of life so we first took  monomer solutions and dried them to form  polyesters  and here's some mass spec data and you  can see that in all cases the um  monomers were able to polymerize into  polymers  let's zoom in again this is one of the  monomers lactic acid so you see that the  products are polydisperse they're not a  single product so you make many  different lengths of polyesters and so  you also form this gel-like structure so  this was  observed in almost all cases where all  of the polymer gel structures  were formed through  by these  poly disperse polyester products so we  took these gels and we dissolved them in  aqueous solution  and what we found was that droplets  could form they were turbid and we could  see these nice spherical droplets in  solution for almost all of these  structures  we actually took these droplets and we  acid them a bit further  so we first found that these droplets  could  you know encapsulate functional  superfold gfp  we also found that these droplets could  catalyze or scaffold the assembly of  some type of lipid structures around the  droplets  and finally we found that rna could  segregate and into and function in the  presence of these droplets  so one of the next questions we tried to  pursue was that you know only one type  of polyester droplet we found could it  could compartmentalize rna but these are  all neutral droplets that we studied  can other polyester droplets or droplets  made from other compositions  compartmentalize rna  and we  hypothesized that if we included a basic  alpha hydroxy acid something that could  be positively charged in solution  the droplets that contain this positive  residue could allow the rna with a  negative backbone to  segregate more strongly than than before  and indeed when we incorporated some  percentage of these cationic residues we  we still saw droplets  but we found that increasing the amount  of cationic residues actually resulted  in disassociation of the droplets and we  actually believe that for example  at really high concentrations of the  cationic residue it's very likely that  the droplets are too polar and too  soluble in water or they're too charged  and too soluble in water so they don't  form the droplets but at lower  percentages  of the cationic residue we still see  droplets and it's very likely that these  droplets are still cationic so it's  likely that the polyester formation is  governed by the total charge of this  mixture  and in fact we did find the ability for  these new novel you know cationic  droplets to segregate rna you can see  here on the bottom that the fluorescent  rna is really strongly segregated into  these droplets here and we found that  even increasing  the amount of  charged monomers  into a neutral polyester by only about  20 percent allowed polyesters which  originally went in the neutral form  couldn't segregate any rna to be able to  segregate some rna here shown in the  middle and the right  so even just a small amount can change  these structures  and so again if you're interested in  learning more details about  this study um or these studies some of  them have been published i would highly  encourage you to read some of these  papers here  um for more details so i'd like to of  course thank my funding sources a lot of  these funding sources came from japan  but also one from the french embassy in  japan  i'd like to thank some of my  collaborators that worked on this  project with me before especially irina  jim kuhan and tommaso both at elsie and  outside of lc  and i'd like to thank other  collaborators at lc and tokyo tech  especially chan and rihanna who recently  have been working with me on some other  really exciting projects related to  polyesters and also a lot of my  collaborators around the world for all  these projects as well it's been a great  experience and i hope to continue  working with everyone  so finally i'd like to give a few  you know  programs that you might be interested in  to get into astrobiology research so  blue marble space institute of science  has a young researcher program over the  summer if you're starting in university  or recently graduated and are interested  to do a virtual project over the summer  you can take a look there are different  projects of different types and  some are research based some are you  know outreach based some are  communication based  and so you can take a look at the  website shown here my main  affiliation elsie we're a research  institute located in tokyo  and we study the origin and evolution of  earth  planets and life on earth  and  here's some other pictures here we've  recently started a five-year integrated  masters phd program  and this program is open to both  domestic and international applicants  and you will be fully funded  if you're interested uh the next  deadline for fall 2022 enrollment is  mid-april you should visit this website  here uh hopefully you'll be interested  in this  and finally uh last year i was part of  the organizing team for a conference  called appgradcon which is astrobiology  graduate conference it's an annual  conference for students and postdocs in  or looking to get into astrobiology and  it's organized by students in post-docs  and it's a really great experience  especially if you've never done a formal  astrobiology or formal presentation it's  a really good  experience and opportunity graham  actually also was  one of the other speakers of this  session was a  an organizer back in the day as well  so the next edition is  online uh it's going to be held in  august and the abstract deadline is may  15th so if you're interested please  visit the website and the the new team  of organizers they've been doing a  really great job and i highly encourage  you to attend that  so thank you all for your time and  attention thank again thanks again to  the organizers for giving me this  opportunity i'm sorry again that i  couldn't be there live with you but i'm  very happy to take questions either by  email  or if you want to leave a comment on the  youtube i'm sure we can be in touch so  thank you again and i hope you enjoy the  rest of this meeting
 

